# etude-5dr plan — caller-cwd runner with fail-closed provenance

## Goal

Ship GitHub issue #15 as one coherent change: a workflow stage may opt its
runner into the CLI caller's repository root, while Etude captures trustworthy
post-run provenance and refuses to capture a caller-workspace output that is not
represented by a clean committed tree. Default stage runners and every gate
continue to execute in the existing detached worktree.

ANCESTOR: etude-5dr (P1) — let Etude produce real work in the caller's live tree
without allowing a gate to pass on provenance measured before that work. The
measurement is the issue's demonstrated silent-failure sequence: manifest commit
X is captured, the runner changes tracked state, and the gate still passes while
reviewing an output that no longer names the live state; another project is
blocked on this capability.

BLAST RADIUS: product changes stay inside this repository/worktree and Etude's
existing refs/etude run records. Verification installs the Etude binary through
the repository's existing install target and creates one temporary Git repository;
the temporary repository is removed after the CLI proof. No host packages,
services, containers, system paths, or shared runtimes are changed.

TOPOLOGY: one Etude process invokes one stage runner at a time. A configured
runner can spawn child processes, but arbitrary same-process code execution is
out of scope. The caller repository may have other external writers, so the
post-run provenance check must reject a changing HEAD rather than assuming sole
ownership.

DURABILITY CLASS: stage capture remains at-most-once per successful CAS append.
The configured runner's external effects are at-least-once across a crash before
capture: a runner that commits and then loses the Etude process may be invoked
again on resume against the new HEAD, exactly as frontier-stage resume works
today. Caller runners must therefore make repeated execution safe. Git commits
are durable and recoverable; Etude captures the resulting HEAD only after the
runner returns. The guard uses the authoritative Git HEAD plus Git's complete
non-ignored working-tree status, not timestamps or a proxy identity.

COMPLETE SET / all-sites enumeration: runner workspace selection occurs at one
stage execution site, `Engine.runAndCaptureStage`. The configuration surface has
two runner sources that both require coverage: a stage-local runner and the
workflow `default_runner`; the CLI `--runner` command override changes the command
only and must retain the stage's effective workspace. Gate check runners, model
seats, replay, bench, retro generation, and standalone `etude gate` are explicitly
outside the new caller-workspace path and remain hermetic/pinned as today.

TRUST BOUNDARY: workflow configuration and the resolved repository root are
trusted operator inputs. Runner output and any tracked-tree mutation performed by
the external runner are untrusted until the post-run Git check succeeds. Git's
resolved HEAD and porcelain tracked-state result are authoritative facts produced
by the repository. Environment values remain governed by the existing allowlist.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCE caller-runner: 1 steps
IRREVERSIBLE STEP caller-runner.1: invoke the configured stage runner in the caller repository

Terminal residual: the runner may have committed while the Etude stage append did
not occur. Git log makes that residual observable and recoverable; resume invokes
the frontier runner again, so the runner owns repeat safety.

## Design

1. Extend `workflow.Runner` and its YAML representation with `workspace`. Accept
   absent/`hermetic` as the current detached-worktree behavior and `caller` as the
   explicit opt-in; reject every other value. Preserve stage-local and
   `default_runner` round trips. Add an effective-workspace helper so command
   resolution and workspace resolution cannot drift.
2. In `Engine.runAndCaptureStage`, choose the runner directory from that effective
   workspace. Hermetic stages continue receiving the existing detached worktree.
   Caller stages receive `Engine.Root`, the repository root resolved from the CLI
   invocation.
3. After a caller runner returns successfully, capture provenance in fail-closed
   order: resolve HEAD, require plain Git porcelain to be empty (including
   untracked but excluding ignored files), resolve HEAD again, and require both
   SHAs to match. This allows a runner to edit and commit, rejects staged,
   unstaged, or new untracked work, and rejects a concurrent commit during
   provenance capture. Put this operation behind an injectable Engine seam so a
   test can deterministically return different first/second SHAs rather than
   relying on a timing race. Only then store the output and append the stage.
   Runner failure/output failure remains an ordinary failed stage and cannot be
   captured.
4. Record `runner_workspace: "caller"` on the manifest stage. Absence continues
   to mean hermetic for existing manifests. Treat this as manifest schema v4:
   preserve parsing of versions 0/2/3, emit v4 only when the new field is present,
   reject the field on older versions, and keep all non-caller manifest bytes and
   version selection unchanged. Render the caller workspace in `run show`.
5. Keep gate wiring unchanged: `runGate` continues receiving the detached
   `worktreeDir` and the existing pinned run SHA. The stage artifact remains the
   review world; caller workspace paths and environment are never passed to a
   gate seat or check.
6. Document the opt-in syntax, post-run committed-clean requirement, recorded
   manifest field, and unchanged gate isolation in `docs/run.md`.

